Cactus Code Thorn ParticleAHFinderX
Core Author(s)  : Jay Kalinani <jaykalinani@gmail.com>
Licence         : GPL-3.0-or-later
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. Purpose
==========

ParticleAHFinderX finds and tracks apparent horizons in CarpetX simulations.
It represents every trial surface with persistent AMReX particles and performs
mesh interpolation, surface geometry, nonlinear relaxation, reductions, and
particle updates with portable accelerator kernels.

Particles are created only when a surface is born and removed only when it is
explicitly retired. They remain in AMReX storage across searches, physical
timesteps, regrids, and checkpoints. Host transfers are restricted to bounded
diagnostics, output, and optional compatibility data.


2. Mathematical Formulation
===========================

ParticleAHFinderX represents one star-shaped closed surface as the radial
graph

  x^i(theta, phi) = C^i + h(theta, phi) n^i(theta, phi),

where C^i is the tracking center and

  n^i = (sin(theta) cos(phi), sin(theta) sin(phi), cos(theta)).

Let e_A^i = partial_A x^i, with A in {theta, phi}. The induced two-metric and
surface area are

  q_AB = gamma_ij e_A^i e_B^j,
  A    = integral sqrt(det(q_AB)) dtheta dphi.

The outward unit normal is obtained from the cross product of the two tangent
vectors and normalized with the inverse spatial metric:

  N_i = epsilon_ijk e_theta^j e_phi^k,
  s_i = N_i / sqrt(gamma^jk N_j N_k),
  s^i = gamma^ij s_j.

With the sign convention used by the thorn, the outgoing-null expansion is

  Theta = D_i s^i + K_ij s^i s^j - K,
  K     = gamma^ij K_ij.

The divergence is evaluated intrinsically on the surface. In particular,

  b_AB       = -s_i (partial_A partial_B x^i
                      + Gamma^i_jk e_A^j e_B^k),
  D_i s^i    = q^AB b_AB.

A marginally outer trapped surface satisfies Theta = 0. The outermost such
surface on a slice is the apparent horizon. The reported irreducible mass and
areal radius are

  M_irr = sqrt(A / (16 pi)),
  R_A   = sqrt(A / (4 pi)).


3. Hyperbolic Relaxation
========================

The nonlinear MOTS equation is solved in an artificial pseudo-time tau. The
surface height h and an auxiliary field v obey

  partial_tau h = v - eta h + Q_h,
  partial_tau v = -Theta    + Q_v,

where eta = (eta*M)/M is set by `eta_damping_times_mass`, the relaxation wave
speed is one, and Q_h and Q_v are optional sixth-order angular dissipation
terms. Dissipation is disabled by default. At a stationary solution without
dissipation, partial_tau v = 0 implies Theta = 0.

The pseudo-time system is integrated with either SSPRK3 or classical RK4. It
is separate from the physical evolution and is independent of the method
selected by ODESolvers. The spacetime remains at one physical time throughout
the search. Metric samples are held fixed across the stages of one pseudo-time
step and gathered again at the updated particle positions after the complete
step.

For each surface, the timestep is

  Delta tau = CFL min_p [h_p min(Delta theta,
                                 sin(theta_p) Delta phi)].

The cell-centered latitude/longitude grid is

  theta_i = pi (i + 1/2) / N_theta,
  phi_j   = 2 pi (j + 1/2) / N_phi,

with N_phi = 2 N_theta. Longitude is periodic. A stencil reflected through a
pole is shifted by pi in longitude. First and second angular derivatives use
centered sixth-order finite differences.

Convergence requires both mass-scaled expansion norms to pass:

  ||Theta||_2   = sqrt[(1/A) sum_p Theta_p^2 w_p],
  ||Theta||_inf = max_p |Theta_p|,

  M ||Theta||_2   <= epsilon_2,
  M ||Theta||_inf <= epsilon_inf,

where w_p is the discrete proper-area weight. Invalid geometry, a missing
interpolation stencil, a non-finite update, or a radius-guard violation fails
that surface and restores its last committed state.


4. Spatial and Angular Discretization
======================================

AMReX position determines a particle's mesh rank, box, and refinement level.
An immutable `(itheta, iphi)` index determines its angular neighbors; particle
storage order is never used as an angular stencil.

The production interpolation path reads the native centering of the ADMBaseX
fields. At every particle it uses one tensor-product 4x4x4 cubic stencil to
gather the six independent components of gamma_ij and K_ij. The eighteen
Cartesian derivatives partial_k gamma_ij are differentiated analytically from
the same cubic polynomial. Trilinear interpolation is retained only as a
bring-up option.

Each point uses the finest valid AMR level that contains the complete stencil
in valid data or synchronized ghost zones. The interpolator never silently
clamps a point or mixes levels within one stencil.

A surface contains exactly N_theta N_phi particles at each angular layer. The
standard target grid is 32x64, or 2,048 particles. With angular continuation,
the persistent 8x16, 16x32, and 32x64 layers are allocated together at surface
birth. A coarse solution is cubically prolonged on the owner GPU; only a
converged target layer is published. A high-frequency adequacy indicator can
promote an under-resolved coarse layer early.


5. Numerical Algorithm
======================

At every synchronized search time, the thorn performs the following steps:

1. Select due surfaces and predict their centers. A tracked surface keeps its
   last successful shape instead of being reset to a sphere.
2. Preflight each proposed surface against the physical domain. Repeatedly
   unsupported surfaces are explicitly retired.
3. Redistribute persistent particles to their AMReX mesh owners and select the
   finest level with a complete interpolation stencil.
4. Gather gamma_ij, partial_k gamma_ij, and K_ij on the device, then route the
   compact samples to the logical owner GPU for each surface.
5. Compute sixth-order angular derivatives of h, the normal, proper-area
   weights, Theta, and the two residual norms.
6. Advance h and v with SSPRK3 or RK4 using the per-surface CFL timestep.
   Radius and finite-value checks are applied atomically per surface.
7. Rebuild particle positions, redistribute as needed, and resample the same
   physical-time geometry after each complete pseudo-time step.
8. Stop converged surfaces immediately. Optionally test recent-trajectory
   extrapolations and accept only those that reduce the normalized residual.
9. For angular continuation, prolong a successful coarse layer and repeat the
   solve through the configured target layer.
10. Atomically commit successful surfaces. Failed searches restore the last
    committed shape and update retry state without publishing a partial result.

All complete logical surfaces are load-balanced across the available GPUs by
particle count. The spatial particles of one surface may still be distributed
over many ranks. Mesh gathering, routing, angular work, and solver reductions
remain device-resident and use GPU-aware MPI in multi-rank accelerator runs.


6. Tracking and Dynamic Common Horizons
=======================================

Manual, external-scalar, centroid, and hybrid center policies are available.
The recommended moving-puncture policy translates the last successful horizon
centroid by the puncture displacement since that solve:

  C_guess(t) = C_AH(t_last) + P(t) - P(t_last).

Pairwise proximity creates only a provisional common-surface candidate; it is
not treated as proof of a merger. Each candidate has persistent particles, a
new stable ID, parent IDs, a bounded radius bracket, and exponential retry
backoff. Promotion requires nonlinear convergence, containment of both
parents, and duplicate rejection. A failed close-fly-by candidate expires
without changing either parent. General unseeded and non-star-shaped MOTS
discovery is not currently implemented.


7. Quick Start
==============

Add `ParticleAHFinderX/ParticleAHFinderX` to the thornlist. A minimal
single-surface configuration is:

  ActiveThorns = "ParticleAHFinderX"

  ParticleAHFinderX::active = yes
  ParticleAHFinderX::solve_surfaces = yes
  ParticleAHFinderX::num_surfaces = 1
  ParticleAHFinderX::find_every = 1

  ParticleAHFinderX::ntheta[0] = 32
  ParticleAHFinderX::nphi[0] = 64
  ParticleAHFinderX::initial_center_x[0] = 0.0
  ParticleAHFinderX::initial_center_y[0] = 0.0
  ParticleAHFinderX::initial_center_z[0] = 0.0
  ParticleAHFinderX::initial_radius[0] = 1.0
  ParticleAHFinderX::mass_scale[0] = 1.0
  ParticleAHFinderX::residual_profile[0] = "dynamic_tracking"

  ParticleAHFinderX::enable_angular_continuation = yes
  ParticleAHFinderX::relaxation_integrator = "ssprk3"
  ParticleAHFinderX::diagnostics_every = 1

For hybrid PunctureTracker motion, additionally set:

  ParticleAHFinderX::center_policy[0] = "puncture_tracker_displacement"
  ParticleAHFinderX::external_center_x[0] = "PunctureTracker::pt_loc_x[0]"
  ParticleAHFinderX::external_center_y[0] = "PunctureTracker::pt_loc_y[0]"
  ParticleAHFinderX::external_center_z[0] = "PunctureTracker::pt_loc_z[0]"

See `param.ccl` for all parameters and `test/` for small regression inputs.


8. Output and Coupling
======================

`diagnostics_every` writes bounded per-surface diagnostics, including stable
IDs, lineage, center, radii, area, irreducible mass, circumferences, expansion
norms, solver steps, and sampled AMR levels. `particle_out_every` writes
parallel openPMD/ADIOS2 BP5 particle snapshots for visualization.

Optional compatibility output provides familiar `BH_diagnostics.ahN.gp`
files and bounded SphericalSurface export. These host-side products are
outside the nonlinear solver loop. Checkpoints preserve particles, logical
surface state, tracking history, candidates, lineage, and output state, and
may be recovered with a different MPI rank count.


9. Current Limitations
======================

- Searches currently run only at fully synchronized CarpetX hierarchy times.
- The supported geometry is one star-shaped S^2 radial graph per surface.
- One logical GPU owns each complete angular surface; a single surface is not
  angularly sharded.
- Only a Cartesian CarpetX patch is currently supported.
- CUDA and CPU/OpenMP builds have been exercised; HIP and SYCL validation is
  still pending.


